UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The 5@C is down one. And the reasoning I was making is if East was strong enough to beat the hand East should have doubled and corrected. The auction the way was done by East did not show that strong a hand. Therefore, West's double really does not make sense in IMPs. |
PG| ***** 89 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In Match Points you might double because 5@C making is likely a bad result anyway. I call that ^*BThe Bad Board^*n theory. In Match Points, if they are making the contract, you are likely to get a bad result. On the other hand, if you think there is a chance you are beating them then you might as well double because you have very little to lose. |
PG| ***** 90 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|But in IMPs the extra few hundred points if they make is worth a lot of IMPs.
|
PG| ***** 91 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BNorth: ^*nE/W are making 4@H. So my 5@C was not a bad sacrifice.|
PG| ***** 92 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|True 5@C was a good bid.

|
