nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is okay if you downgraded to 9 points and bid 3@D if playing |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenraises,Bergen Raises^*N|CP||FP||AT|. If not playing |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenraises,Bergen Raises^*N|CP||FP||AT| then North bids 2@S as a limit raise. |
PG| ***** 97 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The second choice is bidding 3@H to show 4-card support. 2@H and 3@H are not really one of your choices. If not playing |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenraises,Bergen Raises^*N|CP||FP||AT| then you stretch and bid 2@S, particularly with spade shortness.|
PG| ***** 98 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now let us play |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenraises,Bergen Raises^*N|CP||FP||AT| in this auction. So North's choices are between 3@C (10-12) and 3@D (7-9). BTW, a 2@S cue bid the way I play is a 3-card limit raise. Since 3@C shows a 4-card limit raise.|
PG| ***** 99 ***** |


mb|3D|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|3S|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Normally, I would disagree with 3@S since, if partner has 5 spades (most likely length) it is against the law of total tricks with an 8 card fit. However, on this particular deal I like it for a couple of reasons. |
PG| ***** 100 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|First, I will stretch the Law of Total Tricks with a singleton in their suit, second is favorable vulnerability, and third East has a really nice hand. |
PG| ***** 101 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In fact, I wouldn't mind bidding 4@S or 3@H limit raise. Both bids are possible. East choose 3@S which is just competing and has to live with it.|
PG| ***** 102 ***** |


